WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.150
On Earth Day 2019, Google posted this video
about the "urgency of radically addressing

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.070
 

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:16.420
sustainability officer. Now she works to make
Google a global leader in "reducing or even

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:22.240
 

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:28.140
 

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:32.960
by 2017, they got that up to 100 percent.
They've also managed to lower their total

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.850
energy use with some help from a branch of
artificial intelligence called machine learning.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:43.320
A computer program takes in lots of data and
trains itself to operate the centers as efficiently

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:49.969
as possible. Here's energy use under human
supervision, and when the AI is in charge.

00:00:49.969 --> 00:00:54.890
The more data the AI trains on, the better
it gets at reducing energy use. But here's

00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:00.879
the thing: This same technology can be used
to automate lots of other tasks, like fossil

00:01:00.879 --> 00:01:06.380
fuel discovery and extraction. And while Google
is using AI to increase the efficiency of

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:11.659
its operation, it's also using it to try and
get as much oil and gas out of the ground

00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:16.720
as possible. In 2018, Google hired Darryl
Willis, a veteran of British Petroleum, to

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.530
lead its new oil, gas, and energy division.
Willis explained "our plan is to be the partner

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:26.010
of choice for the energy industry." They've
already signed a deal with Total, one of the

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:30.880
world's biggest oil companies, to develop
AI that will streamline oil and gas exploration

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:37.340
and production. And Google's not alone. Microsoft
and Amazon are also teaming up with the fossil

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.880
fuel industry. Big Tech has entered the oil
business.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.880
On June 15, 1957, the citizens of Tulsa, Oklahoma

00:01:53.880 --> 00:02:00.720
buried a rather odd time capsule,
a brand new Plymouth Belvedere. Sealed inside

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:05.540
among commemorative plates, ash trays, t-shirts,
and books that captured the spirit of the

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:11.730
times, was a 16mm film reel. It showed a martian
visiting the United States and learning that

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:17.620
oil and competition made the nation prosper.
Also included in the capsule were gasoline

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.810
and motor oil. In 1957, it seemed like a very real
possibility that these products wouldn't

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:27.840
be around in 50 years when the Belvedere was
scheduled to be disinterred. Newspapers around

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.400
the country were reporting that America's
oil production would soon fall off. And as

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:37.319
that cartoon martian learned, discoveries
of new reserves were rare. "Only one well

00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:46.170
in nine finds any oil at all. And only one
in almost a thousand makes a major discovery."

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:53.650
Oil has always been really hard to find. America's
first oil well was drilled in 1859 near a

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:58.800
particularly greasy creek in Pennsylvania.
It was obvious there was oil seeping up from

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.989
the ground here, but it took Edwin Drake over
a year and all his money to find a measly

00:03:03.989 --> 00:03:09.550
little pocket of black gold. Still, his discovery
triggered an oil boom and a Pennsylvania paper

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:14.250
was soon explaining that the substance could
"illuminate, lubricate, make candles, and cure

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:19.840
most diseases from which humanity suffers."
Not all of that proved true, but 100 years

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:27.360
later, petroleum had given the world "fabrics,
toothbrushes, tires, insecticide, cosmetics,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:36.640
weed killers, a whole galaxy of things to
make a better life on Earth." And of course,

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:43.630
fuel. The energy needs of the world have risen
a lot since 1859. And from very early on there

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:48.220
were fears that fossil fuels couldn't meet
this demand. In 1909, some thought oil and

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:54.629
gas would run out around 1937. In 1937, US
oil supplies were supposed to disappear by

00:03:54.629 --> 00:04:00.390
1952. And so on, and so on, as fossil fuels
became more and more essential in every day

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:06.980
life, predictions of a crash kept coming.
But somehow, the oil kept flowing. "I couldn't

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:13.260
imagine how this ever-increasing supply of
oil was achieved. Until I found out that there's

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:20.150
not just one but thousands of oil companies
all competing with each other to discover

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:26.110
and develop new sources of oil." From the
earliest days, competing companies invested

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:33.020
in better technology to extract fossil fuels,
better drills, better pumps, and they developed

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:38.190
better techniques for finding new oil reserves,
creating seismic vibrations to see underneath

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:45.220
the ground, using satellite LIDAR to reveal
hidden structures, detecting subtle changes

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:50.990
in the Earth's gravity and magnetism. Thanks
in part to these advances, by 2007, when a

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:56.850
very rusty Belvedere was exhumed, global oil
production was still on the rise. In the US,

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:02.090
it did look like oil was finally petering
out, until investment in new technology, fracking

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:07.830
and horizontal wells in vast shale formations,
brought it roaring back. Today, once again,

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:13.139
if we just rely on current reserves and current
tech, oil production will start to falter,

00:05:13.139 --> 00:05:18.620
but if new technology lets us squeeze more
out of the reserves we already have and find

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:23.250
new sources of oil, we'll be able to meet
the growing demand. It's just a matter of

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:29.889
finding the next technological leap. Of course,
there's a problem with using fossil fuels

00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:35.310
to meet the world's energy needs. Climate
change. Oil, gas and coal are a time capsule

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:39.920
of a different sort, sealing ancient carbon
deep below the Earth. When humans open that

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:44.550
time capsule and burn those fossil fuels,
carbon reenters the atmosphere as the greenhouse

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:52.190
gas CO2. Since 1859, CO2 levels have shot
up and so has the planet's temperature. If

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:56.520
we keep going like this, if we burn all the
fossil fuels we currently have access to,

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:02.410
models suggest that the Earth could warm somewhere
between 6.4 and 9.5 degrees. And so climate

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:09.320
activists say there's only one thing to do.
"Keep it in the ground. Keep it in the ground.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:16.210
Keep it in the ground. Keep it in the ground."
That's easier chanted than done. Currently,

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:21.380
the world relies on fossil fuels for 85 percent
of its energy needs. Keeping it in the ground

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:26.610
will require a huge shift to renewables and
lower energy use in general. And big tech

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:31.540
companies have publicly rushed to be part
of this effort. "Sustainability has been a

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:36.350
core value since our founding." "There had
been pockets of sustainability living within

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:40.770
Amazon's business since the very beginning."
"One of the problems we can help solve is

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:45.100
energy consumption." "We can invent our way
out of this problem." "Innovation is the key

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:50.180
to solving this problem." "We can put artificial
intelligence and digital technology to use

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:56.949
to help our customers in every part of the
economy become more sustainable themselves."

00:06:56.949 --> 00:07:02.240
It turns out that same artificial intelligence
technology is just what oil companies need

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:07.610
to stay profitable. See the fossil fuel industry
has amassed lots and lots of valuable data

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:11.830
as they've mapped Earth's crust in search
of reserves. Take this patch of ocean floor

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:17.460
in the North Sea. In 1989, Dutch geologists
painstakingly mapped the different rock layers

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:23.310
using seismic scans. Researchers at IBM recently
fed all that seismic data into a machine learning

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:28.800
algorithm and after about 10 minutes of training,
the AI was able to label the rock layers nearly

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:34.310
as accurately as human experts. Another group
at Georgia Tech used machine learning to quickly

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:39.580
identify structures important to oil discovery.
You could imagine how an AI could train itself

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:44.990
with all kinds of data to pinpoint the best
places to drill. And once drilling begins,

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:49.669
AI can streamline extraction to make it cheaper.
That kind of efficiency can help the oil and

00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:54.009
gas industry compete with renewables and so
it's no surprise that they spent an estimated

00:07:54.009 --> 00:08:01.349
1.75 billion dollars on AI in 2018. Google,
Microsoft, and Amazon are competing for a

00:08:01.349 --> 00:08:06.470
piece of that pie. Google has signed agreements
with several fossil fuel companies. Microsoft

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:11.360
has teamed up with Exxon and Chevron, and
just hired Daryl Willis away from Google.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:16.789
And Amazon, who already provides cloud services
to BP and Shell is marketing its ability to

00:08:16.789 --> 00:08:22.509
accelerate and optimize exploration, drilling,
and production of oil and gas. While they

00:08:22.509 --> 00:08:27.129
talk up their commitment to sustainability,
big tech is making sure the world can keep

00:08:27.129 --> 00:08:36.560
burning plenty of fossil fuel. "And if you
have both of these things, any goal is possible.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:40.449
It's destination unlimited."

